External user textures: a texture another process draws into.

The GPU frame path for render targets (ADR 37). A renderer process draws
into memory the window allocated, and the window paints it with
`paint_texture_quad` (e3) with no copy through the CPU. This adds
`Window::register_external_texture(width, height) -> Result<(UserTextureId,
ExternalTexture), String>` beside `register_dynamic_texture` (c6), a
`PlatformWindow` method whose default answers `unsupported`, and forwards
from the Wayland and X11 windows to the blade renderer, which both use
alike.

On blade the texture comes from `export_texture` in the vendored blade
(patches/blade-graphics/a0): `Rgba8Unorm`, mutable-format with an
`Rgba8UnormSrgb` view, usage transfer source and destination, sampled,
colour attachment and storage, a dedicated device-local allocation
exported as an opaque fd. It's an entry in `user_textures` flagged
`external`: `update_user_texture` and `flush_texture_uploads` skip it,
`release_user_texture` retires it like any dynamic texture, and `destroy`
frees it with the rest. `Window` refuses an update to it as well. The view
the quad samples is narrowed to sampling, because an sRGB view that
inherits the image's storage usage is invalid Vulkan. `ExternalTexture`
carries the fd, the create info as raw Vulkan values, and the device and
driver UUIDs, which is everything an importer needs.

The image leaves `UNDEFINED` once. Registration records that transition
and a release to `VK_QUEUE_FAMILY_EXTERNAL` on an encoder of its own,
submits them, and waits on the window's own fence before the fd is
returned, so a renderer's first write can't race the transition. That is
the only wait, and it is on the window's own work. The window never waits
on anything a renderer controls: no fence, semaphore or mutex is shared
with it, and a paint samples whatever the memory holds at the time.
Registration runs on the UI thread, like every `Window` call. Measured on
RADV (RX 6800 XT) at 1920x1080: 0.7 to 4 ms for the whole call in a
window, across two runs; on an idle queue, 0.55 ms for the export and
0.1 ms for the submit and wait. It happens when a target is made, resized
or moved to another window, not per frame.

Device loss: c5 re-execs the process. The fd is made close-on-exec here,
since a driver needn't, so the restart doesn't carry it into the new
process. The renderer keeps its own import, whose memory lives until it
exits; nothing samples it any more, and if the reset took its device too,
its own next submit says so.

Everything but Linux and FreeBSD answers `Err("unsupported")`, as do the
trait default (macOS, Windows, the test platform) and a backend without
user shaders. A device with no external memory, or one that can't share
this image as an opaque fd, answers with blade's reason. None of these
panic.

gpui's own workspace (b5) patches blade-graphics to the vendored copy as
well, or `cargo test --manifest-path vendor/gpui/Cargo.toml` can't build.
The Cargo.lock the crate ships pins blade 0.7.0, so that run wants
`cargo update -p blade-graphics` first; offline, a lock copied from rox's
resolves where the shipped one doesn't.

Not a backport. A gpui bump carries this forward with c6 and e3, and a
blade bump with blade's a0.

